@* Driving the transcript from outside it. Anything carrying data-scroll-to-message jumps to the
   turn with that MessageId — a menu, an outline, a plain link — which is the markup half of
   shadcn's scrollToMessage and needs no state to do its job.

   The buttons are outside the scroller entirely. That is the point: the command is a name, not a
   reference, so whatever holds the names does not have to be anywhere near the thread. *@
<div class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-sm flex-col gap-4">
    <Card Class="h-140 w-full gap-0">
        <CardHeader Class="gap-1 border-b">
            <CardTitle>Commands</CardTitle>
            <CardDescription>Drive the transcript from outside.</CardDescription>
        </CardHeader>

        <CardContent Class="flex-1 overflow-hidden p-0">
            <MessageScroller OpenAt="end">
                <MessageScrollerViewport>
                    <MessageScrollerContent Class="p-(--card-spacing)">
                        <ChatTranscript Turns="Thread" />
                    </MessageScrollerContent>
                </MessageScrollerViewport>
                <MessageScrollerButton Direction="start" />
                <MessageScrollerButton />
            </MessageScroller>
        </CardContent>

        <CardFooter Class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-2 border-t">
            @foreach (var turn in Thread.Where(t => t.Role == "user"))
            {
                <Button Variant="secondary" Size="sm" data-scroll-to-message="@turn.Id">
                    @turn.Text[..Math.Min(18, turn.Text.Length)]…
                </Button>
            }
        </CardFooter>
    </Card>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-sm px-0.5 text-center text-xs text-balance text-muted-foreground">
        Use the controls to jump to any message in the conversation.
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    static readonly ChatTranscript.Turn[] Thread =
    [
        new("cmd-1", "user", "Can something outside the thread scroll it?"),
        new("cmd-2", "assistant", "Yes, and it needs no handle on the scroller. A button carrying data-scroll-to-message names a turn, and the compiler finds it.\n\nThat is the difference between a name and a reference: the button can live anywhere on the page, including in a menu that is not rendered until it opens."),
        new("cmd-3", "user", "What about a table of contents?"),
        new("cmd-4", "assistant", "The same attribute on every row. An outline is a list of names, and this is a component that answers to names.\n\nNo state is exchanged in either direction, so nothing can fall out of step."),
        new("cmd-5", "user", "And a link from another page?"),
        new("cmd-6", "assistant", "That is what a fragment identifier already does, so the scroller does not need to reinvent it — but the same attribute on a link works too when you want the smooth scroll and the margin rather than the browser's jump."),
        new("cmd-7", "user", "Does it work while the thread is growing?"),
        new("cmd-8", "assistant", "It does. The jump measures the turn at the moment it runs, so it is not affected by anything that arrived before it."),
    ];
}
